    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04668",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "Although the Palaeocene/Eocene thermal maximum is well-documented at low latitudes, a complete lack of quantitative data from the Arctic has prevented scientists from understanding the magnitude of polar warming and evaluating climate model predictions, which consistently underestimate the region's temperatures during this \"greenhouse\" period.",
    "method": "This paper uses the TEX\u2088\u2086' palaeothermometer, an organic geochemical proxy based on archaeal membrane lipids from a marine sediment core, combined with palynological analysis to reconstruct Arctic sea surface temperatures during a past global warming event where traditional proxies for temperature reconstruction are absent.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify Arctic Ocean sea surface temperatures during the Palaeocene/Eocene thermal maximum using a newly recovered marine sediment core, as no data were previously available to assess the Arctic's climate response during this extreme warming event.",
