    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper presents the first quantitative sea surface temperature records from the Arctic Ocean during the Palaeocene/Eocene thermal maximum, revealing subtropical temperatures (~18\u00b0C to >23\u00b0C) that were significantly warmer than climate models had predicted, implying unknown polar amplification mechanisms in greenhouse worlds.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "palaeoclimate models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "underestimate Arctic Ocean summer SSTs by at least 15 \u00b0C for the PETM",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Even if we assume that our TEX86' temperatures represent summer values (see Supplementary Information), palaeoclimate models simulating the early Palaeogene world with 2,000 p.p.m.v. of CO2 in the atmosphere underestimate Arctic Ocean summer SSTs by at least 15 \u00b0C for the PETM and 10\u00b0C for the surrounding late Palaeocene and early Eocene.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "palaeoclimate models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "underestimate Arctic Ocean summer SSTs by 10\u00b0C for the surrounding late Palaeocene and early Eocene",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Even if we assume that our TEX86' temperatures represent summer values (see Supplementary Information), palaeoclimate models simulating the early Palaeogene world with 2,000 p.p.m.v. of CO2 in the atmosphere underestimate Arctic Ocean summer SSTs by at least 15 \u00b0C for the PETM and 10\u00b0C for the surrounding late Palaeocene and early Eocene.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "palaeoclimate models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "pole-to-Equator temperature gradients of ~30\u00b0C",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The models consistently predict pole-to-Equator temperature gradients of ~30\u00b0C (ref. 29). Such gradients represent significant overestimates because they would imply unrealistically warm tropical SSTs considering our polar temperatures.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Arctic Ocean",
            "source_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "relation": "HAS_CLIMATE_ZONE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Tropical",
            "target_entity_type": "ClimateZone",
            "paragraph": "Before the PETM, Apectodinium was a subtropical dinoflagellate restricted to low latitudes\u00b9\u00b9,\u00b9\u2075. Thus, the sudden influx of Apectodinium spp. dinocysts across the PETM at Hole 4A (Fig. 2) suggests a substantial rise in Arctic sea surface temperature (SST) to subtropical or tropical levels.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
